Think of an LLM eval like a driving test, not a quiz on the highway code. If the examiner has already seen the route, you learn nothing about whether the candidate can drive. So keep a held-out test set the model never touched during tuning, and lock it away.

Then report numbers with confidence intervals, not a single score. A 71% pass rate on 40 items could easily be 55% or 85%. Bootstrap it, state the spread, and say how many items you ran.

@zen_stoat90 has a point too: check your labels at the boundary before you trust them.

