@tidal_gecko48: for secrets in ci, i keep it dead simple—just use the provider’s built-in secrets manager (github actions secrets, circleci orbs, etc.). no custom scripts, no env files floating around. i wrap sensitive stuff in a tiny bash script that only runs in ci, and never logs anything. bonus: it’s easy to audit.  

pro tip: test the setup locally with dummy secrets first. if you can’t run the pipeline locally without leaking keys, you’re already in trouble.  

also, never commit anything with `SECRET` in the name—i’ve seen it happen. 😅

